WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.462
This guided practice changes the reference date in the recent respiratory report.

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:07.747
Originally, the report uses August 11, 2026.

00:00:07.747 --> 00:00:13.527
Now move the reference date to August 5, 2026 and think about how the thirty-day window changes.

00:00:13.527 --> 00:00:16.727
Before you run the query, identify admissions near the edges of the date window.

00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:18.532
Those are the records most likely to move in or out.

00:00:18.532 --> 00:00:25.172
Notice that changing the reference date changes the cohort, but it does not change the age threshold used

00:00:25.172 --> 00:00:28.372
by the CASE expression. The WHERE clause defines which rows are included.

00:00:28.372 --> 00:00:33.417
The CASE expression classifies the rows that survive.

00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:36.647
This separation is useful in reporting systems.

00:00:36.647 --> 00:00:36.952
Imagine a weekly dashboard.

00:00:36.952 --> 00:00:43.212
The reporting window may move every week, while the definition of High Risk remains the same.

00:00:43.212 --> 00:00:47.462
Or the opposite may happen: the cohort stays fixed, but the classification policy changes.

00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:52.482
When debugging a changing result, ask which part changed: population definition, calculated measure,

00:00:52.482 --> 00:00:57.047
or classification rule? Run the revised query and compare the admissions with the original version.

00:00:57.047 --> 00:01:02.084
Focus on records near the date boundaries rather than scanning every row equally.
